[pose_scale] bbox stays raw pixels either way.
  A raw feature with a larger range moves the weights more than a 0..1 feature.
  [0] bbox_relative: x,y inside the current bbox (about 0..1). Raw bbox then dominates the LSTM.  <-- default
  [1] absolute: x,y in image pixels, same units as raw bbox. Also leaks where the person stands.
[feeder] interactive_pick_pose_scale <- '0'

[pose_min_height] pose of small pedestrians:
  Reason: below about 40 px the person is a few dozen pixels tall. YOLO then either finds
  nobody (all-zero vector) or guesses a default upright skeleton, sometimes with high
  confidence. Both cases carry almost no body-motion information, and the frame is
  strongly tied to distance from the camera, which bbox already encodes.
  [0] keep pose for ALL frames (current behaviour, comparable with earlier runs)  <-- default
  [1] zero pose when bbox height < 40 px
  [2] custom threshold
[feeder] interactive_pick_pose_min_height <- '1'
[config] POSE_SCALE = bbox_relative  POSE_MIN_HEIGHT = 40
